WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
The chain rule.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:04.562
This single rule is how a network learns.

3
00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:06.967
Let's see how rates of change multiply.

4
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:09.900
Imagine a chain of two steps.

5
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.233
Start with x.

6
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:13.733
If u changes twice as fast as x.

7
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:19.919
And y changes three times as fast as u, then y changes six times as fast as x.

8
00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.333
Rates of change simply multiply.

9
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:25.667
Here is the rule for nested functions.

10
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:29.833
Take the slope of the outer function, evaluated at the inner value.

11
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.867
Then multiply by the slope of the inner function.

12
00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:35.700
Every layer contributes one local factor.

13
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.300
Let's test it in Python.

14
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.300
The function is the sine of x squared, nested.

15
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.767
By the chain rule, the slope is cosine of x squared, times two x.

16
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.432
We compare against a tiny nudge, and the answer is True.

17
00:00:49.432 --> 00:00:50.333
The rule holds.

18
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:54.967
A neural network is nothing but deeply nested functions.

19
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:56.800
The loss wraps a softmax.

20
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.533
Which wraps the last layer.

21
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:00.699
Which wraps the one before it.

22
00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:05.300
Backprop multiplies local derivatives from the outside in, layer by layer.

23
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.339
Now a real debugging story.

24
00:01:08.339 --> 00:01:12.833
A twelve layer CNN with sigmoids stopped learning after epoch two.

25
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:16.048
Sigmoid's slope is at most zero point two five.

26
00:01:16.048 --> 00:01:21.567
Multiply that through twelve layers and you get roughly six times ten to the minus eight.

27
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:24.334
The early layers' gradients had vanished.

28
00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:28.367
ReLU has slope one for active units, and skip connections fixed it.

29
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.733
All of that was chain rule arithmetic.

30
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:32.695
Let's recap.

31
00:01:32.695 --> 00:01:35.233
Rates of change multiply through layers.

32
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:38.533
Backpropagation is the chain rule applied backward.

33
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:42.533
Long chains of small factors vanish, and large ones explode.

34
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:44.333
Next up: gradients.
